    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Wal">Wal</ref> 1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="1" corresp="#wit-h"><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.star-2"/><pb n="392" ed="#liebermann"/>Walreaf is niðinges dǽde<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.1"/>: gif hwá ofsacen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.2"/> wille, do þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> mid eahta ⁊ feowertig fulbórenra þegena.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.3"/></ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="1" corresp="#wit-q"><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.star-3"/><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.1-2"/><pb n="393" ed="#liebermann"/>Wealreaf<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.2-2"/> (id est mortuum refare<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.3-2"/>) est opus niðingi:<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.4"/> si quis<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.5"/> negare uelit, faciat hoc<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.6"/> cum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.7"/> (XLVIII tainis plene nobilibus<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.8"/>.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.1"/><pb n="393" ed="#liebermann"/>Leichenberaubung<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.2"/> ist That eines [Fried- und] Ehrlosen:<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.3"/><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de1.star"/> wenn es einer ableugnen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.4"/> will, thue er das mit [Eideshilfe von] 48<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.5"/> vollgeborenen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Wal_part-001.lnote.de.6"/> Thegnas.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Robbing a corpse is the deed of a nithing [a man without peace or honour]: if anyone wishes to deny it, let him do so with [the oath-help of] 48 fully-born thegns.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#unattributed" corresp="#wit-h">Robbing the slain is a villianous act; if anyone wishes to deny it, let him do so with [the help of] 48 noble-born thegns.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="372"><label>possibly related</label> <ref target="clause:IIIAtr_7">III Atr 7–7.1</ref> <quote>might the Wantage provision that corpses of executed thieves could be 'taken up' … (III 7 – 7:1), conceivably have been construed as a licence</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="372"><label>matches</label> <ref target="clause:IIIAtr_13">III Atr 13</ref> <quote>the Wantage demand that accusations … be cleared with 'three twelves' match the Walreaf stipulation that this outlaw's deed be answered with an oath of forty-eight</quote></note>
        <note type="editorial">U: translator unrecorded: check; The Latin is the Quadripartitus; Liebermann's Old English is from H (Textus Roffensis f. 32v, after Ordal, same hand, c. 1120; perhaps a fragment of a law, after c. 910 because of Norse influence). Liebermann explains ‘niðing’ as ‘worthless, wretched man’. In the Quadripartitus it follows Pax; IV Atr follows it. K: a stray opening bracket before ‘XLVIII’ removed. Clause number 1 added (this text has a single clause). Liebermann compares I Cn 3 and 6.2a (MS A).; F: corrected against Liebermann, Gesetze I (date at the head of the text); Wormald, Making of English Law I; Whitelock, English Historical Documents I, October 2026 (was “c.910–c.1060 (Liebermann); ?c.995 (Wormald)”, now “c.910–c.1060 (Liebermann, I p. 392); ?c.995 (Wormald, MEL pp. 371–2)”); BQ: page of each of Liebermann’s texts and of his German, checked against Liebermann, Gesetze I (our double-read transcription), October 2026 (e.g. was “(empty)”, now “H 392; Q 393; German 393”; 1 rows of Wal)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.star" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">hinter</hi> Pax, <hi rend="italic">o. S.</hi> 391</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">after Pax, above p. 391</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">392</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl. I Cn.</hi> 3, <hi rend="italic">A</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. I Cn. 3, A</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">392</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">vgl.</hi> healden <hi rend="italic">I Cn.</hi> 6, 2<hi rend="italic">a, A</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cf. healden I Cn. 6, 2a, A</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">392</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">folgt</hi> II As., <hi rend="italic">o. S.</hi> 150.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">there follows II As., above p. 150.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.1-2" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Überschrift aus MHk, schon vor</hi> Wif; Item <hi rend="italic">R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Heading from MHk, already before Wif; Item R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Tuel. <hi rend="italic">R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Tuel. R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.3-2" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-hínd <hi rend="italic">MHk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-hínd MHk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Twifhyndi <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Twifhyndi T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ex — den. <hi rend="italic">fehlt R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ex — den. missing in R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">XV <hi rend="italic">statt</hi> ex V <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">XV instead of ex V T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">natus <hi rend="italic">statt</hi> n. e. <hi rend="italic">Hn</hi> 76, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">natus instead of n. e. Hn 76, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.8" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-dia <hi rend="italic">Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-dia Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.star-2" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">392</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Textus Roffensis f.</hi> 41<hi rend="italic">ᵛ</hi>, <hi rend="italic">in derselben Zeile und Hand um</hi> 1120, <hi rend="italic">hinter</hi> EGu., <hi rend="italic">oben S.</hi> 134, <hi rend="italic">Sp.</hi> 1, <hi rend="italic">Anm.</hi> 18 [<hi rend="italic">Bruchstück eines Gesetzes?</hi>]</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Textus Roffensis f. 41ᵛ, in the same line and hand, c. 1120, after EGu., above p. 134, col. 1, note 18 [fragment of a law?]</seg><note type="remark" resp="#claude">Liebermann’s note on the head of the column (the manuscript)</note></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.oe.star-3" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">hinter</hi> Wif</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">after Wif</seg><note type="remark" resp="#claude">Liebermann’s note on the head of the column (the manuscript)</note></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Laut des Nordischen und der Lesart H braucht man nicht mit Schmid 673 die Schreibung <hi rend="italic">wæl</hi> einzuführen; das altagsä. <hi rend="italic">wælreaf</hi> heisst nicht ‘verbrecherischer Leichenraub’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">In view of the Norse and of the reading of H, there is no need to introduce the spelling <hi rend="italic">wæl</hi> with Schmid 673; the old A.-S. <hi rend="italic">wælreaf</hi> does not mean ‘criminal robbery of corpses’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">refare</hi> setzt für agsä. <hi rend="italic">reafian</hi> Q Iudex 10, vielleicht aus Fränk. Recht</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q puts <hi rend="italic">refare</hi> for A.-S. <hi rend="italic">reafian</hi> in Iudex 10, perhaps from Frankish law</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. Gl</hi> Nithing</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See Gl</hi> Nithing</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de1.star" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">393</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Nichtswürdigen’, Elenden</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘Worthless man’, wretch</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nordisch <hi rend="italic">afsaka</hi> ‘to exculpate’ vergleicht Toller</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Toller compares Norse <hi rend="italic">afsaka</hi> ‘to exculpate’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. Gl</hi> Eideshelfer 18b. 47</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See Gl</hi> Eideshelfer 18b. 47</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Wal_part-001.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">230</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Wort belegt Toller nur hierher (<hi rend="italic">vgl. fullæðele</hi> ‘sehr edel’); es bed. bei Langobarden ‘ehelich’ (so <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> Bastard 8; Schwerin <hi rend="italic">Grundr. Gesch. wiss</hi>. V 92), bei Friesen ‘von zwei adeligen Eltern’; Richthofen <hi rend="italic">Untersuch. Fris. RG</hi> II 1095; ‘<hi rend="italic">volboren, bene natus</hi> ist nur der Edle’; E. Mayer <hi rend="italic">Dt. Frx. Vfg</hi>. I 39; auch Toller versteht ‘nobleborn’; <hi rend="italic">s. Gl</hi> Thegn 11a</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Toller's only instance of the word is this passage (<hi rend="italic">cf. fullæðele</hi> ‘very noble’); among the Lombards it means ‘legitimate’ (so <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> Bastard 8; Schwerin <hi rend="italic">Grundr. Gesch. wiss</hi>. V 92), among the Frisians ‘born of two noble parents’; Richthofen <hi rend="italic">Untersuch. Fris. RG</hi> II 1095; ‘only the noble man is <hi rend="italic">volboren, bene natus</hi>’; E. Mayer <hi rend="italic">Dt. Frx. Vfg</hi>. I 39; Toller too understands ‘nobleborn’; <hi rend="italic">see Gl</hi> Thegn 11a</seg></note>
      </div>
